1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,680
The gloss command is the way in to everything else.

2
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:06,280
Point it at a folder, and it lints.

3
00:00:07,030 --> 00:00:10,810
It reads the whole of each path, and reports every finding.

4
00:00:11,370 --> 00:00:13,870
Each one prints in the format a compiler uses.

5
00:00:14,290 --> 00:00:19,610
So Xcode shows it inline, a build server can read it, and a commit hook can

6
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:20,150
stop on it.

7
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:24,020
Two options narrow the lint to a change.

8
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:26,660
Staged judges the next commit.

9
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:30,000
Since takes a revision, and judges your branch against it.

10
00:00:30,658 --> 00:00:35,818
Either way, the whole change is read, so links into untouched files still resolve.

11
00:00:36,458 --> 00:00:41,378
But only findings on declarations the change added or changed are reported.

12
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:46,280
The second job is to export the interface.

13
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:50,770
Give it a folder and the interface command writes JSON.

14
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:54,110
With no file named, it goes to standard output.

15
00:00:54,370 --> 00:00:56,510
Or you can ask for a web page instead.

16
00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:03,200
The JSON holds the files read, one contract for every declaration, and the graph of how

17
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:04,060
they mention each other.

18
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:09,520
Beside the contracts sit the lint's assessments, one for each, at the same index.

19
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:15,030
The reading is done once, and the contracts and the lint meet only here.

20
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:20,430
The shape carries a schema number, now two, so other tools know when it changes.

21
00:01:22,433 --> 00:01:24,713
The third job is the story check.

22
00:01:25,153 --> 00:01:30,733
Every branch is meant to explain why it changed things, in a story file named for

23
00:01:30,733 --> 00:01:31,133
the branch.

24
00:01:31,883 --> 00:01:37,883
The story command finds where the branch left its base, and compares the interface then with

25
00:01:37,883 --> 00:01:38,703
the interface now.

26
00:01:39,203 --> 00:01:43,243
Every type that changed must appear in the story, with a note of its own.

27
00:01:43,993 --> 00:01:45,793
It prints what is missing.

28
00:01:46,133 --> 00:01:47,433
Types not in the story.

29
00:01:47,793 --> 00:01:49,013
Types with no note.

30
00:01:49,473 --> 00:01:50,613
Links that name nothing.

31
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:53,093
The exit status says the rest.

32
00:01:53,093 --> 00:01:55,333
Zero for a complete story.

33
00:01:55,333 --> 00:01:58,253
One for gaps, or no story at all.

34
00:01:58,253 --> 00:02:00,473
Two when git cannot answer.

35
00:02:02,467 --> 00:02:04,107
The last job is the bench.

36
00:02:04,567 --> 00:02:08,487
It does the work the app does for a package, and times each phase.

37
00:02:09,237 --> 00:02:12,657
It reads the files, builds the map, narrows the list as you type,

38
00:02:12,977 --> 00:02:16,257
lays out every level, and works on the pages of the busiest types.

39
00:02:16,677 --> 00:02:17,957
Then it prints a table.

40
00:02:18,077 --> 00:02:21,637
For each phase, how many times it ran, how long it took in all,

41
00:02:21,797 --> 00:02:23,037
and its slowest run.

42
00:02:23,297 --> 00:02:27,537
A slow phase shows at a glance, and a change that slows one shows against an

43
00:02:27,537 --> 00:02:28,097
earlier run.

44
00:02:28,791 --> 00:02:31,631
The command also keeps its promises to other tools.

45
00:02:31,871 --> 00:02:36,391
The lint exits with one only when there are errors, so a style can bring a

46
00:02:36,391 --> 00:02:37,771
rule in as a warning first.

47
00:02:38,011 --> 00:02:40,171
A missing value gives usage status.

48
00:02:40,451 --> 00:02:42,611
A broken style file stops the run.

49
00:02:42,811 --> 00:02:47,131
And a path that does not exist is an error, so a typo cannot pass as

50
00:02:47,131 --> 00:02:47,711
a clean run.
